# TODO - CPU-first C-to-Malbolge release

## Objective

Ship a reproducible CPU-only C-to-Malbolge command that compiles a useful
documented deterministic-C subset and proves real guest execution without
waiting for native acceleration, CUDA, DOOM, self-hosting or publication.

## Acceptance

- Complete the exact five acceptance-evidence groups in the owning contract
  using a tracked compound source with mutable locals, input-dependent
  branching, arithmetic and an EOF-driven loop.
- Run generated code on the normative Malbolge VM against independent
  C23 defined-behavior and binary stream oracles; no host-output substitution.
- Reproduce from a clean checkout with pinned tools and a single compiler
  command, including negative/no-partial-output cases.
- Record the exact supported subset and limits rather than claiming arbitrary
  C, and retire this TODO only after the release evidence is durable.
- **Finite acceptance witness:** Exactly the five owning release-evidence
  groups, two distinct tracked C programs, five pinned binary input classes and
  two C23 oracle optimization modes on one declared Linux host; bound
  compilation and guest VM steps.

## Evidence

- Not yet established. Existing source/guest experiments authenticate bounded
  byte-preserving statements but do not implement general guest runtime C
  storage, dependent branching or EOF-driven loops.
- This is an explicit earlier delivery target than the final cross-platform
  turnkey compiler. It does not alter that contract's dependencies.
